# The site is pure static HTML + assets/. Everything below is local tooling or
# source data that the deployed pages never request at runtime, so keep it out
# of the deployment bundle.

# Content-generation scripts (Python/Node) — run these locally, not on Vercel.
build/

# Source + intermediate data. The pages read assets/products.js, which is
# generated from these and committed, so the originals are not needed at runtime.
data/
*.csv

# Docs and machine-specific editor state.
*.md
.claude/

# Internal-only pages. Neither is linked from the site, and both were reachable
# by anyone who guessed the URL — and indexable.
#   plan   — the V2 planning document: budgets, revenue targets, risk register
#   review — the Facebook parse QA tool, which fetches data/ (excluded above),
#            so it deployed as a broken page that still exposed internal notes
texas_truck_parts_v2_plan.html
fb-review.html

# Image-fetcher bookkeeping. Both are written by the download scripts to record
# what has already been pulled, and nothing at runtime reads them — no <img>,
# no fetch, no reference anywhere in assets/products.js. 267 KB of build state
# that was being served to the public.
assets/img/manifest.json
assets/img/rh/manifest.json

# NOTE on assets/img/_facebook/ — 224 of the 344 files in there (10.5 MB) are
# orphans from earlier scrapes that no product references any more, and it is
# tempting to list them here. Deliberately NOT doing that:
#
#   - The runtime saving is zero. Nobody requests a file no page links to, so
#     excluding it speeds up no page view; it only trims the deploy bundle.
#   - The risk is not zero. The used/orphaned split is decided by whatever
#     assets/products.js currently references, and that changes every time the
#     catalogue is regenerated. A hardcoded list of 224 filenames would go stale
#     silently and start excluding images that HAD become live — which shows up
#     as broken product photography in production, not as a warning.
#
# If the 10.5 MB is worth reclaiming, delete the orphans from the repo in a
# deliberate pass (they are re-downloadable via `npm run fb:images`) rather than
# hiding them behind an ignore list that has to be kept in sync by hand.
